A validator checks every AI summary against facts extracted from the original notice. If it fails, the pipeline retries once with specific feedback, then falls back to the baseline. A user can always override. This is a hybrid of both implementation options in the brief:

Rule-based: the notice checklist (audience, date, action, next step) and the fact extraction.
API-integrated: AI generation, with validation, logging, and user override.
Repo structure
notice-summarizer/
├── notice_tool/
│   ├── __init__.py
│   ├── ingest.py          # upload -> clean text
│   ├── text.py            # normalize, sentence splitting
│   ├── facts.py           # dates, times, money, phones, emails, URLs
│   ├── baseline.py        # extractive summary
│   ├── checklist.py       # rule-based check of the INPUT notice
│   ├── validator.py       # accuracy checklist: summary vs notice
│   ├── llm_summarizer.py  # prompt + structured output schema
│   └── pipeline.py        # orchestration, retry, fallback, logging, override
├── app.py                 # Streamlit demo
├── evaluate.py
├── samples/               # sample notices (+ expected facts)
├── tests/
├── logs/                  # gitignored runtime logs
├── docs/
│   ├── decisions.md
│   ├── evaluation.md
│   └── demo.md
├── .env.example
├── .gitignore
├── requirements.txt
└── README.md
Step 1: Setup

Same secrets-first order as before: .gitignore (with .env, .venv/, logs/, __pycache__/) is committed before any .env exists.

powershell
mkdir notice-summarizer
cd notice-summarizer
git init
python -m venv .venv
.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
pip install langchain-core langchain-openai pydantic python-dotenv streamlit pytest

requirements.txt with floors and ceilings (UTF-8): I tested langchain-core 1.8.4, langchain-openai 1.8.5, pydantic 2.x, streamlit 1.67, so use langchain-core>=1.8,<2, langchain-openai>=1.8,<2, pydantic>=2,<3, streamlit>=1.40, python-dotenv>=1.0, pytest>=8.

.env.example: OPENAI_API_KEY, OPENAI_MODEL, and an optional OPENAI_BASE_URL, placeholders only.

Step 2: Sample notices

Create samples/*.txt with at least six synthetic notices. Never use real notices containing children's names or phone numbers. Include these deliberate traps:

A normal notice with a date, a time, an amount, a phone number, and an email (an excursion or PTA meeting)
A date range ("from 7 to 18 December")
Several amounts and times, with different formats ("₦8,500", "N8,500", "7:30 a.m.", "2.30 PM")
A prohibition ("Do not send children with phones")
A notice with a wrong weekday (for example "Monday, 24 November 2026", which is actually a Tuesday). Check weekdays with datetime.date(2026, 11, 24).strftime("%A").
A vague notice with no date or contact ("come to school soon")
An informational notice with no action to take

For each notice, write what a good summary must keep. This is your ground truth.

Step 3: facts.py (write it test-first)

This is the main challenge, so start with the tests. Write the extractor until this table passes. I wrote my version against exactly these cases, and they all pass.

Dates should come out as (month, day) pairs:

Input	Expected
24 November 2026 / 24th November / Tuesday, 24 Nov.	{(11, 24)}
November 24th, 2026 / Nov. 24	{(11, 24)}
24/11/2026 / 24.11.2026	{(11, 24)} (day first)
8 to 12 December / 8-12 December 2026	{(12, 8), (12, 12)}
1st December and 15th of January	{(12, 1), (1, 15)}
We resume on the 5th	{} (no month, not a full date)
Room 12 to 15 pupils	{}

Times should come out as 24-hour strings:

Input	Expected
8:00am / 8 a.m.	08:00
2.30 PM / 14:30	14:30
9am to 1pm	09:00, 13:00
12pm / 12am	12:00 / 00:00

Money should come out as (currency, amount):

Input	Expected
₦15,000, N15,000, NGN 15000, 15,000 naira	("NGN", 15000.0)
₦2,500.50	("NGN", 2500.5)
$20, £5	("USD", 20.0), ("GBP", 5.0)
Class N2 has 30 pupils	{}
Nursery N2 and N1	{}
N500, N5,000	accepted
₦50	accepted (the symbol is unambiguous)

That N2 row is a real decision. A bare "N" before a number could be naira or a class name, so I required three or more digits or a thousands comma after a bare "N". The "₦" symbol and "NGN" accept any amount. Write that rule and your reasoning in docs/decisions.md.

Phones: 0803 123 4567, 08031234567, 0803-123-4567, 080-3123-4567, +234 803 123 4567, +2348031234567 should all become 8031234567. Numbers like 0123456789 or Room 0803 must not match. Match any grouping of the right number of digits.

Emails (lowercased) and URLs (trailing full stop removed) are matched too.

Two limits I found, which you should test and document as known limits:

27/11 (day/month with no year) is not recognised. A summary that abbreviates a date that way is flagged as dropping it. That's a false alarm, which is the safe direction for a tool protecting dates.
Day-first is assumed for numeric dates. Say so.
Step 4: baseline.py, text.py, checklist.py

Sentence splitting needs to protect abbreviations, because a naive split breaks "8 a.m. sharp" and "Mrs. Adeyemi" and "24 Nov. 2026". Protect a.m., p.m., Mr./Mrs./Dr., No., and month abbreviations. Also split on line breaks and bullets. Known limit: a sentence ending in "a.m." isn't split from the next one, which is safe for preservation.

Extractive baseline:

Keep every sentence that contains a date, time, amount, or contact, or an instruction word (must, please, bring, pay, do not, deadline and so on).
Add the opening sentence if it fits the word budget.
Return the kept sentences in their original order.
Report the length reduction and whether the result went over budget.

Preservation beats brevity here, and that is the decision to explain in your README.

Rule-based notice checklist: for the input notice, each check returns ok, weak, or missing, with a suggestion from a fixed list:

audience: does it say who it's for?
date: an exact date is ok. A relative one ("tomorrow", "soon") is weak, because summaries lose relative dates.
action: is there an instruction?
next step: a contact, or a phrase like "reply" or "submit"?
weekday matches date: when a notice gives a weekday plus a full date with a year, compare them with Python's datetime.
Step 5: validator.py, the accuracy checklist

Compare a summary to its source. Each check returns pass or fail plus a detail:

Check	What it tests
dates_preserved, times_preserved, money_preserved, contacts_preserved	Every fact in the notice appears in the summary, in any format
no_invented_facts	The summary adds no date, time, amount, or contact that isn't in the notice. A date that's only partly changed is caught here
prohibitions_kept	If the notice forbids something, the summary contains a negation
evidence_quotes_found	Each action's quoted evidence appears in the notice (after normalising whitespace and quotes)
shorter_than_notice	For notices over about 80 words, the summary is at most about 70% of the length

Comparing normalised facts rather than raw strings matters. A summary that says "Dec 3rd, 7.30am, N8,500, 080-3123-4567" must still pass.

Tests to write: a good summary passes, each changed fact is caught (date, time, amount, phone), an invented fact is caught, a dropped prohibition is caught, fake evidence is caught, an over-long summary is caught, and equivalent formats pass.

Step 6: llm_summarizer.py

Use a structured output so the model must give you checkable pieces:

python
class ParentSummary(BaseModel):
    headline: str
    summary: str
    key_dates: list[KeyDate]      # what + when
    actions: list[ActionItem]     # action + evidence (exact words from the notice)

The prompt rules are the part to write carefully: keep every date, time, amount, phone, and email exactly as written, keep every prohibition, add nothing that isn't in the notice, copy the supporting words into evidence, and be much shorter. Build the chain with PROMPT | llm.with_structured_output(ParentSummary). On a retry, add the validator's failure message to the prompt.

Privacy note for your README: only send approved, synthetic, or anonymised text to a third-party API. Real notices can contain personal details.

Step 7: pipeline.py

The flow:

Reject bad input with a clear error: empty, too short (under five words), too long (over about 3,000 words).
Compute the checklist and the baseline.
If there's no model, return the baseline with status extractive_only.
Otherwise generate, validate, and on failure retry once with the failure text.
If it still fails, or the API errors, fall back to the baseline with status fallback_extractive.
Log every failure to a JSONL file: timestamp, a short notice hash, event, and the details. Don't log the full notice text.
Override: apply_override(result, source, edited_text) stores the user's version, runs the validator on it, and logs the override with whether it passed. The user keeps the final say, but the system records when they overrode a failed check.

Catching broad Exception at the model call is deliberate here, because any model failure should lead to the safe fallback. Log the exception type.

Step 8: ingest.py and the app

Upload: read .txt and .md as UTF-8 (fall back to another encoding if it fails), reject other types and oversized files, and normalise line endings. PDF and Word support is a stretch goal. Test the upload logic directly, because I verified Streamlit's tester with text inputs and buttons only, not the file-upload widget.

Streamlit app (keep it thin, with all logic in notice_tool): a text area plus uploader, a Summarise button, the checklist with suggestions, the extractive and AI summaries side by side, the validator results, an editable final text, and a "Use my edited version" button. Use st.session_state to keep the result between clicks. Test it with streamlit.testing.v1.AppTest:

python
at = AppTest.from_file("app.py").run()
at.text_area(key="notice").set_value(text)
at.button[0].click().run()
Step 9: Tests (the "tested core flows" requirement)

Write these before submitting. All use a scripted stand-in for the model, never the real API:

python
class ScriptedLLM:
    def __init__(self, *outputs): self.outputs, self.prompts = list(outputs), []
    def with_structured_output(self, schema):
        def run(prompt_value):
            self.prompts.append(prompt_value.to_string())
            out = self.outputs.pop(0)
            if isinstance(out, Exception): raise out
            return out
        return RunnableLambda(run)

Core flows to cover:

the baseline never loses a fact on every sample notice
a good AI summary passes validation on the first attempt
the AI drops a date, the retry prompt contains the failure text, and the second attempt passes
the AI fails twice and the pipeline falls back to the baseline
an API error falls back and is logged
an invalid notice is rejected
an override is applied and logged
the log file is valid JSON lines
Step 10: Evaluation

evaluate.py runs each sample through the baseline (and the AI path if you have a key) and writes docs/evaluation.md with a table: words before and after, reduction percentage, fact recall (what share of dates, times, amounts, and contacts were preserved), and whether validation passed.

Explain why you use fact recall and not a text-overlap score like ROUGE. Those scores reward fluent wording, but they can't tell you that "3 December" became "4 December". My baseline numbers on my own samples are above, so you can sanity-check yours. Run real AI summaries only if you have a working key, and label any simulated results as simulated.

Step 11: Short demo

Record a one-to-two-minute screen recording (or a GIF with screenshots) and link it from the README. A good storyline:

Upload a notice and show the checklist catching a vague or wrong-weekday notice.
Show the extractive baseline.
Show an AI summary passing validation.
Show a failure: the validator catches a dropped date, retries, or falls back.
Show a user override and the log entry it produced.

To show a failure reliably without depending on a live model, add an optional "simulate a faulty AI" switch to the app that uses the scripted stand-in, and say so in the demo.

Step 12: docs/decisions.md and the README

The brief wants a README explaining decisions. Cover:

why both approaches (baseline safe but barely shorter, AI shorter but risky)
why validate facts instead of trusting the model, and why compare normalised facts instead of raw text
the bare-"N" money rule, the day-first date assumption, and the dd/mm-without-year false alarm
why the pipeline retries once, then falls back
why users can override, and why overrides are logged
limitations: it checks that facts are present, not that the wording is faithful. It can't tell if the summary changes the meaning of an instruction, and negation checking is a rough heuristic. Add: "validators reduce risk, they don't remove it"

The README also needs setup, how to run the app (streamlit run app.py), how to run tests (pytest), the model and key configuration, the privacy note, and a file table.

